Title: "Selected Open Problems"
Id: "8ac6237e-b34e-4667-8a7a-1fd9950c7476"


Text: "A conjecture belongs in a mathematical library only when its statement is
       precise and its unproved status is unmistakable. The examples below are
       not included as curiosities. Each arises naturally from structures
       developed earlier, and each illustrates the gap that can remain between
       overwhelming evidence and proof. Verification of finitely many cases,
       however extensive, cannot establish a universal assertion about all
       integers or all complex zeros."
Id: "c929b171-e961-4640-a453-7e459851f114"


SectionTitle: "Additive and Multiplicative Structure of Primes"
Id: "67c5e464-3e6e-40d6-9da8-2be12e536dc9"


TextDefinition: "A positive integer $p>1$ is prime if its only positive divisors
                 are $1$ and $p$. Every integer greater than $1$ factors as a
                 product of primes, uniquely up to the order of the factors.
                 This multiplicative classification does not make additive
                 questions about primes easy."
Id: "133119f5-9771-4e7e-b84e-de640884b36e"


TextConjecture: "Strong Goldbach conjecture. Every even integer greater than $2$
                 is the sum of two prime numbers. The order of the two primes is
                 irrelevant, and equality is allowed; for example, $10=3+7=5+5$.
                 The conjecture is a universal existence claim, so computation
                 can verify initial ranges but cannot by itself settle all even
                 integers."
Id: "1945a57b-f397-4f58-b515-fd8878910f2e"


TextConjecture: "Twin prime conjecture. There exist infinitely many primes $p$
                 for which $p+2$ is also prime. Such pairs are called twin
                 primes. Results proving infinitely many prime pairs within a
                 fixed bounded distance do not by themselves prove that the
                 distance $2$ occurs infinitely often."
Id: "565f765c-47cd-427f-a5b5-44c87d1a9794"


SectionTitle: "Zeros of the Zeta Function"
Id: "1ce51384-d4ed-4ff8-ae1f-1a249702737c"


TextDefinition: "For complex $s$ with $\operatorname{Re}(s)>1$, the Riemann zeta
                 function is $\zeta(s)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n^{-s}$. It admits a
                 meromorphic continuation to $\mathbb{C}$ with a single simple
                 pole at $s=1$. Its zeros at the negative even integers are
                 called trivial; all other zeros lie in the critical strip
                 $0<\operatorname{Re}(s)<1$."
Id: "6afb5d40-7bb1-43d7-b2e1-c4d5691fe031"


TextConjecture: "Riemann hypothesis. Every nontrivial zero $\rho$ of the Riemann
                 zeta function satisfies $\operatorname{Re}(\rho)=1/2$. The
                 hypothesis gives sharp control over the error in the average
                 distribution of prime numbers and is substantially stronger
                 than the prime number theorem itself."
Id: "2315cd43-c1d4-4518-bb73-1e2aa34b2df0"


Text: "These conjectures differ in language but share a methodological lesson.
       Definitions and known theorems constrain where a counterexample could
       occur; computation and partial results supply evidence; only a proof can
       promote the conjecture to a theorem. A future resolution should change
       the card's status rather than silently changing the meaning of the
       statement."
Id: "3d6d34ce-b17e-42c2-9293-d605cb268841"
